{"id": "live-382", "title": "Illinois Appellate Court Holds Commercial Landlords May Assign Claims for Tenants' Unpaid Rent", "category": "lease-disputes", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-11-09", "jurisdiction": "Appellate Court of Illinois, First District", "state": "IL", "amount": "No dollar figure stated", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/resources/1c390993-0f1d-4885-a638-bb93fbb2a028/file", "summary": "The Appellate Court of Illinois, First District, held on November 9, 2026, that a commercial landlord may assign its claim for a tenant's already-accrued, unpaid rent to a third party, reversing a Cook County circuit court that had dismissed a rent-collection suit against a Chicago commercial tenant for lack of standing. The circuit court had relied on an 1872 Illinois Supreme Court decision and a 2018 First District opinion for the proposition that claims for back rent do not transfer when assigned separately from the property. The appellate panel found that reasoning inconsistent with the modern Illinois law of assignable claims and remanded the case for further proceedings on the merits of the unpaid-rent claim.", "significance": "The ruling confirms that accrued-but-unpaid rent is a freely transferable asset distinct from the property that generated it, meaning a commercial landlord selling or exiting a troubled tenancy can monetize uncollected rent receivables separately rather than losing the ability to collect them. It is also a due-diligence flag for buyers of leased commercial property: absent a specific assignment in the purchase agreement, a new owner has no claim to rent that accrued before closing, and the right to pursue a delinquent tenant for that older rent stays with the seller or whoever the seller assigns it to.", "judge": null, "tags": ["commercial-lease", "unpaid-rent", "assignment", "illinois", "standing"]}
